<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jupiter (god)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jupiter was the chief god of the ancient Roman state religion, god of the sky and thunder, identified with the Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. As Jupiter Optimus Maximus he was worshipped with Juno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a> in the great temple on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitoline Hill</a>, begun under the last kings and, by tradition, dedicated in 509 BCE. Victorious generals ended their triumphs at his temple, and his cult spread throughout the Roman provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jupiter-god.jpg" alt="Jupiter (god)" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Jupiter_-_Louvre_MR_254_-_Louvre-Lens_(2).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
